      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000123292,
      "SummaryText": "EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\nFOR THE 2025 RE-SUBMISSION OF THE\nMAGNA Water District Waste Water Treatment Plant\nRISK MANAGEMENT PLAN\n\nThis Risk Management Plan (RMP) sets forth the procedures, methods and equipment used to prevent releases of chlorine from the Magna Water District (the District), Wastewater Treatment Plant (WWTP), located at 7650 West 2100 South in Magna, Utah 84044.  The RMP is required per the provisions of Part 68 of Title 40 of the Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), because chlorine is used in the WWTP process in a quantity exceeding the 2,500 pound threshold listed in 40CFR 68.130.\n\nThe RMP identifies processes from which a potential chlorine release may occur.  In addition, the RMP describes the practices that are being used to reduce the likelihood of accidents and communicates compliance with the terms and conditions of the Chemical Accident Prevention Provisions set forth in 40CFR Part 68.\n\nAccidental Release Prevention and Emergency Response Policies\nProcess safety and emergency response procedures have been implemented at the District\u0092s WWTP to minimize or eliminate the impacts from emergencies and/or accidents.  Emergency response plans are fully reviewed and supported by the District\u0092s management and the RMP coordinator.\n\nThe Stationary Source and Regulated Substances Handled\nThe District\u0092s WWTP is a sewage treatment plant that treats raw sewage to secondary effluent standards.  Chlorine is used at the District\u0092s WWTP to disinfect the effluent. The chlorine is stored as a liquid under pressure in four one-ton cylinders.  There are no other chemicals stored at the site above the threshold quantities listed in 40CFR 68.130.  \n\nAccidental Release Prevention Program\nThe potential for discharges of hazardous substances from this facility is minimal.  The District\u0092s WWTP strictly adheres to good housekeeping, materials management practices and training practices to prevent incidents.  All releases are to be mitigated immediately.  Materials and equipmen"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000123292,
      "SummaryText": "t necessary for prompt spill clean-up are kept on-site.  \n\nFive-year Accident History\nThe facility has not experienced a significant release, spill or leak of any toxic pollutant, or hazardous material, or experienced an injury as result of a release in the past five years.\n\nEmergency Response Program\nAll aspects of the Emergency Response Program have been coordinated with Unified Fire Authority.  If a release occurs, employees have been trained to take corrective and clean-up actions and notify the RMP coordinator so that they can determine if the spill should be reported to the appropriate agency."
    }
  ]
